# Telegram-poller-isolation fix — the three §1b handsoff-loop launch sites

The verified kill vector: an internal loam `claude` that loads the
telegram plugin spawns a competing `bun server.ts` that SIGTERMs a
concurrently-running operator session's single-consumer Telegram
poller for the one bot-token getUpdates slot. Removing the telegram
plugin from the spawned process's reachable set (empty strict-MCP
config) + scrubbing the bot-token/API-key env is necessary AND
sufficient operator-protection (proven + sealed under AC.LIPW.5/.6
on the subloam-driver surface).

Source-verified scope correction: the ONLY unisolated `claude`-spawn
family in this loam copy is the handsoff-loop (§1b). The §1a sites
(subloam-driver / upgrade-merge-resolver / workspace-sync resolver
client / odd-extractor synthesis client) already carry
byte-equivalent isolation under their own sealed ACs; the §1c sites
are non-`claude` subprocesses. This amendment closes ONLY the §1b
family — more contained than the root-cause report's prose implied.

Surface changed:
  - framework/tools/handsoff-loop/src/handsoff_loop/_isolation.py —
    a thin helper that imports the PROVEN subloam-driver functions
    (build_isolated_claude_argv / build_isolated_env /
    write_empty_mcp_config / IsolationConfig) and produces, for the
    §1b sites' existing `-p`/json argv shape, the isolation-flag
    injection (--strict-mcp-config --mcp-config <empty>) + scrubbed
    env + empty-MCP file lifecycle. No new isolation machinery.
  - intake.py:_claude_json, goal_drive.build_goal_drive_argv,
    orchestrator._dispatch_subagent — each spawned `claude` now
    carries the empty-strict-MCP isolation + scrubbed env. The
    existing argv shape (positional `-p` prompt, --output-format
    json, --permission-mode bypassPermissions) is preserved; only
    the isolation flags + env scrub are added — not a reshape.
  - framework/tools/handsoff-loop/tests/test_AC_TPI_*.py — one file
    per AC. AC.TPI.1/.2 opt-in real-binary poller-survives
    (sentinel alive after a real spawn — empirical survival is the
    proof); AC.TPI.3/.4 fast structural argv/env sentinels in the
    seal sweep; AC.TPI.5 marker-guard regression durability;
    AC.TPI.6 fence integrity (§1a/§1c untouched).

